  "Question": "The product of \\(\\sqrt[3]{4}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[4]{8}\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt[7]{12}\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\sqrt[7]{12}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt[7]{32}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt[12]{32}\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\sqrt[12]{32}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Because \\(4=2^2\\) and \\(8=2^3\\), both radicals can be written with the same positive base. Multiplying then adds their exponents:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{4}\\sqrt[4]{8}\n=2^{2/3}2^{3/4}\n=2^{8/12+9/12}\n=2^{17/12}.\n\\]\nSince \\(17/12=1+5/12\\), we can separate one factor of \\(2\\):\n\\[\n2^{17/12}=2\\cdot2^{5/12}\n=2\\sqrt[12]{2^5}\n=2\\sqrt[12]{32}.\n\\]\nAll bases are positive, so these fractional-power rules apply. The answer is \\(2\\sqrt[12]{32}\\), choice E.",
